-- Facts restated in Boston Daily's own words with attribution. Every story carries its own photo; none was used in an earlier package or in the open 2026-11-09 draft (grepped content/ and seed/).
-- loc.gov and Wikimedia Commons were unreachable from the newsroom sandbox on this run. Photo URLs are Library of Congress URLs built from resource IDs confirmed by search (Carol M. Highsmith, no known restrictions; one Prints and Photographs item flagged for rights confirmation). Editor: confirm each photo loads and matches its caption; the image check removes any that do not.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261115-faa-ground-stop', 'faa-nationwide-ground-stop-duffy-outdated-air-traffic-system', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'policy', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'A four-hour nationwide ground stop halts U.S. flights as Duffy blames an outdated air traffic control network', 'The FAA stopped every departure in the country on Friday, Nov. 13, after radar and radio outages at regional control centers. Officials saw no sign of a cyberattack, and Democrats in Congress are demanding answers.', 'The Federal Aviation Administration halted all departures across the United States on the afternoon of Friday, Nov. 13, after radar and communications problems hit several regional air traffic control centers. The ground stop was issued at 3:54 p.m. Eastern time and lifted at 8:15 p.m., about four hours and 20 minutes later, ABC News reported. It was only the second nationwide ground stop since the Sept. 11 attacks; the last one came in January 2023, when an FAA computer system failed.

## What went wrong

The FAA said it acted "for reasons of safety." Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy blamed an overloaded and outdated air traffic control network, saying switches and routers carrying its data became overwhelmed and produced inconsistent information, according to ABC News. He said investigators were still working out the exact cause. Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin said there was no indication of a cyberattack so far, though officials had not ruled one out.

## The toll

Duffy said about 3,500 planes were in the air when the order was issued. Inbound flights were allowed to land. FlightAware counted nearly 7,000 U.S. flights delayed or canceled by late afternoon, MPR News reported, and hundreds of flights were diverted. The disruption reached into college football on Saturday, Nov. 14: Utah did not reach Tucson until just after noon and its game at Arizona was pushed back three hours, and Ole Miss at Oklahoma was moved to Sunday, Nov. 15, according to ESPN and NCAA.com.

## The politics

Duffy called the situation an emergency and urged Congress to approve billions of dollars to modernize the system, warning that similar outages would happen again without it. He said he expected the system to be fully operating before Thanksgiving travel. House Democratic Leader Hakeem Jeffries called the outage an unprecedented breakdown, Sen. Tammy Duckworth said she would press the FAA for answers, and Sen. Maria Cantwell said the Senate Commerce Committee would review the communications failures, ABC News reported.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://tile.loc.gov/image-services/iiif/service:pnp:highsm:15400:15447/full/pct:25/0/default.jpg', 'Dulles International Airport, Virginia (file). Photo: Carol M. Highsmith / Library of Congress (highsm.15447), no known restrictions', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-15.sql', 9, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-15T10:45:00.000Z', '2026-11-15T10:45:00.000Z');
